<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A light aircraft is flown on a course of <m>{195}^\circ</m> at an airspeed of <m>{180}</m> knots. A wind of <m>{60}</m> knots blows from <m>{235}^\circ</m>.</p><p>(a) Find the ground speed, correct to 1 decimal place.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans1"/> knots  </p><p>(b) Find the drift angle, correct to 1 decimal place.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/> <m>^\circ</m>  </p><p>(c) Find the track, as a bearing correct to 1 decimal place.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans3"/> <m>^\circ</m>  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>A wind blowing from <m>{235}^\circ</m> pushes the aircraft towards <m>0{55}^\circ</m>. Draw the course arrow (<m>{180}</m> knots towards <m>{195}^\circ</m>), then the wind arrow from its tip (<m>{60}</m> knots towards <m>0{55}^\circ</m>). The angle between the two directions is <m>{140}^\circ</m>, so the angle inside the triangle between the arrows is <m>{40}^\circ</m>.</p><p>(a) By the cosine rule, ground speed<m>^2 = {180}^2 + {60}^2 - 2 \times {180} \times {60} \cos {40}^\circ</m>, so the ground speed is <m>{139.476} \approx {139.5}</m> knots.</p><p>(b) By the sine rule, <m>\sin d = \dfrac{ {60} \sin {40}^\circ}{ {139.476} }</m>, so <m>d = {16.052}^\circ \approx {16.1}^\circ</m>.</p><p>(c) The wind pushes the aircraft anticlockwise of its course, so the track is <m>{178.9}^\circ</m>.</p><p>Check with components (east, north): <m>\begin{pmatrix} {2.6} \\ {-139.5} \end{pmatrix}</m>, whose length is the ground speed and whose bearing is the track.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>139.475589365</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>16.0523887324</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>178.947611268</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>